1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:03,858
Peru, the coast, one of the driest deserts on Earth.

2
00:00:03,858 --> 00:00:10,366
And buried in the sand here is a ring of stone that may be five thousand five hundred years old.

3
00:00:10,833 --> 00:00:12,471
Older than Stonehenge.

4
00:00:12,471 --> 00:00:14,853
Older than the pyramids of Giza.

5
00:00:14,853 --> 00:00:21,033
Some websites say it was built by a lost civilization, or by visitors from the sky.

6
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:23,788
So tonight we keep a dig diary.

7
00:00:23,788 --> 00:00:29,766
Day by day, trench by trench, we ask what is really in the ground at Sechin Bajo.

8
00:00:30,233 --> 00:00:30,795
Day one.

9
00:00:30,795 --> 00:00:31,356
Arrival.

10
00:00:31,356 --> 00:00:33,533
Let us begin with where we are.

11
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:39,540
And what they found here in two thousand eight changed the timeline of the Americas.

12
00:00:39,540 --> 00:00:40,200
Or did it?

13
00:00:44,666 --> 00:00:45,529
Chapter one.

14
00:00:45,529 --> 00:00:46,104
Arrival.

15
00:00:46,104 --> 00:00:57,466
The Casma valley lies in the Ancash region of northern Peru, about three hundred kilometres north of Lima, and about twelve kilometres from the Pacific coast.

16
00:00:57,933 --> 00:00:59,947
Here it almost never rains.

17
00:00:59,947 --> 00:01:02,409
Less than ten millimetres a year.

18
00:01:02,409 --> 00:01:08,900
Life hugs the river, in narrow green oases that cut across the sand like green threads.

19
00:01:09,366 --> 00:01:12,194
Fifty-seven rivers cross the Peruvian coast.

20
00:01:12,194 --> 00:01:18,299
Each one carried water to the desert, and each valley became a place where people could settle.

21
00:01:18,766 --> 00:01:22,616
Today the valley holds a cluster of ancient sites.

22
00:01:22,616 --> 00:01:23,617
Cerro Sechin.

23
00:01:23,617 --> 00:01:24,541
Sechin Alto.

24
00:01:24,541 --> 00:01:25,774
Taukachi-Konkan.

25
00:01:25,774 --> 00:01:30,933
And the one we came for, Sechin Bajo, on the far bank of the river.

26
00:01:31,400 --> 00:01:36,340
The first big name here is the Peruvian archaeologist Julio Tello.

27
00:01:36,340 --> 00:01:44,799
On the first of July, nineteen thirty-seven, he and Toribio Mejia Xesspe found the carved stones of Cerro Sechin.

28
00:01:45,266 --> 00:01:48,830
Tello thought it was the capital of a whole culture.

29
00:01:48,830 --> 00:01:51,571
We now call it the Casma-Sechin culture.

30
00:01:51,571 --> 00:01:56,299
And for decades, the story stopped there, around sixteen hundred B C.

31
00:01:56,766 --> 00:02:02,079
Then, in the nineteen-nineties, a team started digging deeper at Sechin Bajo.

32
00:02:02,079 --> 00:02:03,666
Deeper than anyone had.

33
00:02:04,133 --> 00:02:05,015
Chapter two.

34
00:02:05,015 --> 00:02:05,823
Trench one.

35
00:02:05,823 --> 00:02:10,673
The team was led by the German-Peruvian archaeologist Peter Fuchs.

36
00:02:10,673 --> 00:02:17,066
Their method was the oldest one there is: dig a square, record each layer, and go down.

37
00:02:17,533 --> 00:02:19,941
Each layer is a page in a book.

38
00:02:19,941 --> 00:02:21,340
Ash from a hearth.

39
00:02:21,340 --> 00:02:23,049
Sand blown in by wind.

40
00:02:23,049 --> 00:02:24,525
Clay, laid by hand.

41
00:02:24,525 --> 00:02:27,400
The deeper you go, the older it gets.

42
00:02:27,866 --> 00:02:30,937
Under the later buildings, they found a wall.

43
00:02:30,937 --> 00:02:31,414
Curved.

44
00:02:31,414 --> 00:02:34,621
Made of stone, and of rectangular adobe bricks.

45
00:02:34,621 --> 00:02:38,033
Older than the pyramid they were standing next to.

46
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:42,593
As they cleared the sand, the curve closed into a circle.

47
00:02:42,593 --> 00:02:46,400
A sunken circular plaza, ten to twelve metres across.

48
00:02:46,866 --> 00:02:47,678
Not a house.

49
00:02:47,678 --> 00:02:48,489
Not a grave.

50
00:02:48,489 --> 00:02:49,504
A public space.

51
00:02:49,504 --> 00:02:56,200
A place where people gathered, and where someone planned the shape before the first stone was laid.

52
00:02:56,666 --> 00:02:58,457
The stones are not giant.

53
00:02:58,457 --> 00:03:01,321
They are fitted by hand, bound with mud.

54
00:03:01,321 --> 00:03:05,833
This matters, because the internet loves to call it megalithic.

55
00:03:06,300 --> 00:03:12,655
Real building needs real people: someone to carry stone, someone to mix mud, someone to plan.

56
00:03:12,655 --> 00:03:17,166
That is already a society, even if we know nothing about its name.

57
00:03:17,633 --> 00:03:18,554
Chapter three.

58
00:03:18,554 --> 00:03:19,014
Dating.

59
00:03:19,014 --> 00:03:21,184
How do we know how old a wall is?

60
00:03:21,184 --> 00:03:22,500
We ask the charcoal.

61
00:03:22,966 --> 00:03:25,483
Every living thing takes in carbon.

62
00:03:25,483 --> 00:03:29,437
When it dies, a tiny part of it decays at a known rate.

63
00:03:29,437 --> 00:03:31,666
A lab can measure what is left.

64
00:03:32,133 --> 00:03:38,600
At Sechin Bajo, the samples came from burned plant material in the layers of the plaza.

65
00:03:38,600 --> 00:03:42,466
Radiocarbon put them around thirty-five hundred B C.

66
00:03:42,933 --> 00:03:46,043
That is about fifty-five hundred years ago.

67
00:03:46,043 --> 00:03:53,133
Five hundred years older than Caral, the famous city of Peru, built around twenty-six hundred B C.

68
00:03:53,599 --> 00:03:58,283
Older than the Great Pyramid of Giza by roughly nine hundred years.

69
00:03:58,283 --> 00:04:00,800
Older than Stonehenge's first ditch.

70
00:04:01,266 --> 00:04:02,113
But careful.

71
00:04:02,113 --> 00:04:04,936
Radiocarbon gives a range, not one date.

72
00:04:04,936 --> 00:04:10,510
And the team itself announced these results in two thousand nine, in a journal.

73
00:04:10,510 --> 00:04:13,333
Not everyone has seen the full data yet.

74
00:04:13,800 --> 00:04:21,485
The report: Fuchs and colleagues, Boletin de Arqueologia, Catholic University of Peru, two thousand nine.

75
00:04:21,485 --> 00:04:24,266
It is a real paper, and a careful one.

76
00:04:24,733 --> 00:04:30,300
Some Andean specialists say: promising, but we need more dates from more layers.

77
00:04:30,300 --> 00:04:31,204
That is fair.

78
00:04:31,204 --> 00:04:34,266
One strong claim needs more than one trench.

79
00:04:34,733 --> 00:04:35,743
Chapter four.

80
00:04:35,743 --> 00:04:36,597
The frieze.

81
00:04:36,597 --> 00:04:44,366
Nearby, the team also reported a stone frieze, two metres tall, dated around thirty-six hundred B C.

82
00:04:44,833 --> 00:04:50,646
If that date holds, it would be some of the earliest monumental art in the Americas.

83
00:04:50,646 --> 00:04:53,000
Before any pottery in this region.

84
00:04:53,466 --> 00:04:55,075
That is the odd thing.

85
00:04:55,075 --> 00:04:58,293
At this time, people here did not make pots.

86
00:04:58,293 --> 00:05:01,657
Archaeologists call it the pre-ceramic period.

87
00:05:01,657 --> 00:05:03,266
Yet they built plazas.

88
00:05:03,733 --> 00:05:04,840
They had baskets.

89
00:05:04,840 --> 00:05:07,054
They had cotton, and fishing nets.

90
00:05:07,054 --> 00:05:10,311
They fished on the coast and farmed in the valley.

91
00:05:10,311 --> 00:05:12,199
No pots, but great buildings.

92
00:05:12,666 --> 00:05:18,246
Now compare Cerro Sechin, just across the river, around sixteen hundred B C.

93
00:05:18,246 --> 00:05:24,633
A wall of upright granite slabs, carved with warriors, and the bodies of their victims.

94
00:05:25,100 --> 00:05:29,259
Dramatic art, almost two thousand years after Sechin Bajo.

95
00:05:29,259 --> 00:05:32,988
These are the carvings that appear in documentaries.

96
00:05:32,988 --> 00:05:36,000
They are not the oldest part of the story.

97
00:05:36,466 --> 00:05:39,476
Which is a trap for the casual viewer.

98
00:05:39,476 --> 00:05:41,298
Cerro Sechin is famous.

99
00:05:41,298 --> 00:05:44,466
Sechin Bajo is older and almost unknown.

100
00:05:44,933 --> 00:05:45,847
Chapter five.

101
00:05:45,847 --> 00:05:46,901
The neighbours.

102
00:05:46,901 --> 00:05:49,502
Look at Sechin Alto, right beside it.

103
00:05:49,502 --> 00:05:55,899
A temple platform three hundred and fifty metres by three hundred, thirty-five metres high.

104
00:05:56,366 --> 00:05:58,912
Five plazas stretch two kilometres.

105
00:05:58,912 --> 00:06:05,166
The whole complex covers three to four hundred hectares: five times the size of Caral.

106
00:06:05,633 --> 00:06:09,127
Inside, large stones alternate with small ones.

107
00:06:09,127 --> 00:06:11,728
Conical adobe bricks fill the core.

108
00:06:11,728 --> 00:06:14,033
Careful, ordered, planned work.

109
00:06:14,500 --> 00:06:17,955
Tello called it the largest building of the Americas.

110
00:06:17,955 --> 00:06:23,433
So when someone says: the people here were primitive, ask them to stand at its foot.

111
00:06:23,899 --> 00:06:33,500
The region also links to Caral, Norte Chico, a chain of early cities that grew along the Peruvian coast, with no writing and no wheels.

112
00:06:33,966 --> 00:06:36,091
So Sechin Bajo is not alone.

113
00:06:36,091 --> 00:06:43,300
It is the oldest piece of a big landscape, one that grew in complexity over two thousand years.

114
00:06:43,766 --> 00:06:47,160
And much of it is still under farmland and sand.

115
00:06:47,160 --> 00:06:50,766
Whole zones around Sechin Alto have never been dug.

116
00:06:51,233 --> 00:06:52,155
Chapter six.

117
00:06:52,155 --> 00:06:52,923
The claim.

118
00:06:52,923 --> 00:07:01,833
Search online, and you will find posts saying Sechin Bajo is proof of a lost civilization, or of ancient astronauts.

119
00:07:02,300 --> 00:07:04,556
The argument is always the same.

120
00:07:04,556 --> 00:07:05,121
Too old.

121
00:07:05,121 --> 00:07:05,685
Too big.

122
00:07:05,685 --> 00:07:06,531
Too perfect.

123
00:07:06,531 --> 00:07:08,999
So someone else must have built it.

124
00:07:09,466 --> 00:07:10,983
Take them one by one.

125
00:07:10,983 --> 00:07:11,561
Too old?

126
00:07:11,561 --> 00:07:14,016
The dates are real, from charcoal.

127
00:07:14,016 --> 00:07:17,699
Hammer stones, baskets, and mud were all this took.

128
00:07:18,166 --> 00:07:19,023
Too perfect?

129
00:07:19,023 --> 00:07:20,238
A circle is easy.

130
00:07:20,238 --> 00:07:23,666
A cord, a stake, and a person walking around it.

131
00:07:23,666 --> 00:07:26,666
Archaeologists have found cords like this.

132
00:07:27,133 --> 00:07:28,267
Anything alien?

133
00:07:28,267 --> 00:07:29,023
No object.

134
00:07:29,023 --> 00:07:29,703
No metal.

135
00:07:29,703 --> 00:07:30,535
No machine.

136
00:07:30,535 --> 00:07:32,047
No unexplained tool.

137
00:07:32,047 --> 00:07:37,566
Not one thing in the trench that people of that time could not have made.

138
00:07:38,033 --> 00:07:42,264
What remains is a feeling, that ancient people could not have done it.

139
00:07:42,264 --> 00:07:45,166
That feeling says more about us than about them.

140
00:07:45,633 --> 00:07:55,100
And it is also quietly unfair to the real builders, the people of the Peruvian coast, who invented monumental architecture on their own.

141
00:07:55,566 --> 00:07:56,528
Chapter seven.

142
00:07:56,528 --> 00:07:57,490
Who were they?

143
00:07:57,490 --> 00:07:59,207
Honestly, we do not know.

144
00:07:59,207 --> 00:08:02,024
We have no name, no language, no writing.

145
00:08:02,024 --> 00:08:03,466
Only what they built.

146
00:08:03,933 --> 00:08:08,925
Their food was anchovies from the sea, and beans and squash from the valley.

147
00:08:08,925 --> 00:08:12,866
Their trade was cotton, which they spun into nets and cloth.

148
00:08:13,333 --> 00:08:16,166
The circular plaza suggests rituals.

149
00:08:16,166 --> 00:08:17,268
A shared fire.

150
00:08:17,268 --> 00:08:18,449
A shared story.

151
00:08:18,449 --> 00:08:21,833
Maybe a ruler, maybe a council, maybe both.

152
00:08:22,300 --> 00:08:27,033
Some scholars think early temples here tracked the sun and the seasons.

153
00:08:27,033 --> 00:08:29,699
That is a theory, with hints, not proof.

154
00:08:30,166 --> 00:08:33,178
We only know about one hundredth of the site.

155
00:08:33,178 --> 00:08:36,190
A future trench could change the story again.

156
00:08:36,190 --> 00:08:38,466
That is the thrill of this season.

157
00:08:38,933 --> 00:08:41,741
Back in the trench, nothing is thrown away.

158
00:08:41,741 --> 00:08:50,166
Every bucket of sand goes through a sieve, because the smallest thing, a bead, a seed, a fish bone, can tell us how people lived.

159
00:08:50,633 --> 00:08:54,743
From those scraps, the team can count what people ate.

160
00:08:54,743 --> 00:08:55,504
Anchovies.

161
00:08:55,504 --> 00:08:56,341
Sea snails.

162
00:08:56,341 --> 00:08:56,874
Squash.

163
00:08:56,874 --> 00:08:59,766
A diet of the coast, not of the stars.

164
00:09:00,233 --> 00:09:04,993
And every layer is drawn by hand, to scale, before it is removed.

165
00:09:04,993 --> 00:09:07,923
Once a layer is dug, it is gone forever.

166
00:09:07,923 --> 00:09:10,266
The drawing is all that is left.

167
00:09:10,733 --> 00:09:12,257
Evening in the camp.

168
00:09:12,257 --> 00:09:12,639
Wind.

169
00:09:12,639 --> 00:09:13,934
Dust in the lamp.

170
00:09:13,934 --> 00:09:20,566
This is the part of archaeology that documentaries skip: the slow, careful, quiet work.

171
00:09:21,033 --> 00:09:23,633
One of the quiet treasures is cloth.

172
00:09:23,633 --> 00:09:28,327
Woven cotton, preserved by the dry desert for thousands of years.

173
00:09:28,327 --> 00:09:31,000
Ordinary people, with ordinary skill.

174
00:09:31,466 --> 00:09:33,806
The valley is still farmed today.

175
00:09:33,806 --> 00:09:36,925
The same river, the same sand, the same sun.

176
00:09:36,925 --> 00:09:41,533
That continuity is why the ground here keeps its secrets so well.

177
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:46,854
Some videos claim the site is far older, tens of thousands of years.

178
00:09:46,854 --> 00:09:51,566
There is no radiocarbon date, no layer, and no paper that says so.

179
00:09:52,033 --> 00:09:58,681
A good rule from the field: the further a claim goes beyond the data, the more careful you must be.

180
00:09:58,681 --> 00:10:00,158
Charcoal can be dated.

181
00:10:00,158 --> 00:10:01,300
A feeling cannot.

182
00:10:01,766 --> 00:10:03,426
So here is the surprise.

183
00:10:03,426 --> 00:10:06,261
The real story is stranger than the myth.

184
00:10:06,261 --> 00:10:12,000
People with no pottery and no writing built public circles of stone, and it spread.

185
00:10:12,466 --> 00:10:20,355
And the same idea, the sunken circular plaza, appears again and again along the coast, in Caral and beyond.

186
00:10:20,355 --> 00:10:23,600
A tradition, passed on for a thousand years.

187
00:10:24,066 --> 00:10:28,699
Today drones, ground radar and new dates are mapping the buried parts.

188
00:10:28,699 --> 00:10:33,066
Every year, the picture gets a little sharper, and a little older.

189
00:10:33,533 --> 00:10:36,371
And that is what a diary like this is for.

190
00:10:36,371 --> 00:10:43,333
Not to find a shortcut to an answer, but to watch slowly, honestly, as people stand up out of the sand.

191
00:10:43,799 --> 00:10:45,666
Time to score Sechin Bajo.

192
00:10:46,133 --> 00:10:49,673
A circular plaza of stone and mud: evidence.

193
00:10:49,673 --> 00:10:53,777
Radiocarbon near thirty-five hundred B C: evidence.

194
00:10:53,777 --> 00:11:00,133
Oldest monumental building in the Americas: partial, since it needs more dates.

195
00:11:00,600 --> 00:11:04,538
A lost civilization or alien builders: no evidence.

196
00:11:04,538 --> 00:11:07,627
Impossible for ancient people: debunked.

197
00:11:07,627 --> 00:11:09,866
Who they were: still unknown.

198
00:11:10,333 --> 00:11:11,267
The verdict.

199
00:11:11,267 --> 00:11:12,902
Real, and remarkable.

200
00:11:12,902 --> 00:11:17,495
Sechin Bajo is one of the oldest monuments in the Americas.

201
00:11:17,495 --> 00:11:19,519
And no aliens were needed.

202
00:11:19,519 --> 00:11:23,100
Just people, mud, stone, and a very good idea.

203
00:11:23,566 --> 00:11:29,461
Five and a half thousand years ago, someone pressed a stone into wet clay and began a circle.

204
00:11:29,461 --> 00:11:31,933
We are still reading what they started.

205
00:11:32,399 --> 00:11:40,899
Next case: a stone city in Africa, a name that means houses of stone, and a question that colonial writers tried to bury.

206
00:11:41,366 --> 00:11:43,041
Would you have kept digging?

207
00:11:43,041 --> 00:11:48,066
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
